\section{Match a signed-offset load, then check the boundary}

A toy target offers \texttt{LD32off}, which reads a 32-bit word at a 64-bit base plus a sign-extended six-bit immediate. Address addition wraps modulo \(2^{64}\); the plain load and the offset load have identical alignment, byte order, fault and memory-order behavior at the same address. The pattern covers the address addition and the load, accepts a base and a constant, and returns the loaded value. It does not produce the computed address. The trace elides memory-state edges, which are preserved by the replacement.

\begin{verbatim}
a = add64(base, 12);  x = load32(a);  y = xor64(a, mask)
d = add64(base, 40);  z = load32(d)
c = add64(base,-16);  w = load32(c)

# Anchor at loads; the address port fixes the producer.
x: {load -> x, add -> a},  12 in [-32,31] : candidate
z: {load -> z, add -> d},  40 not in range : reject
w: {load -> w, add -> c}, -16 in [-32,31] : candidate

# Consumed definitions with external users need an output.
x: reject this cover choice; y still needs a
w: select if the other cover constraints hold; c has no other use
\end{verbatim}

There are three structural embeddings at the anchors and two after the immediate guard. Only the one at \(w\) can enter a no-duplication cover of this graph, assuming \(y\) is live and there are no other patterns for \(y\) that absorb the address calculation. For \(x\), a plain load and a separate add preserve \(a\); for \(z\), they also avoid misencoding 40. A matcher dedicated to a no-duplication selector can reject the \(x\) embedding early once it knows the external use cannot be satisfied. A more general implementation can report the boundary obligation and let the selector resolve it. In either case every live use must still have a definition. Counting structurally matched operations alone would miss that invariant.

\Cref{fig:visual-275} makes the two load embeddings that pass the immediate check explicit. Only one can hide its address computation.

